        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/id": "f38d491a-6dbf-5ea6-9e1d-28ccc7170e0f",
        ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186083240/plots/d7f3b05ffc420dbca0eff6.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=6\\left(2t{e}^{t}\\right),\\,y=4\\left({t}^{2}+2t\\right),\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(t)}{{x}^{\u2032}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=6\\left(2{e}^{t}(t+1)\\right),\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=4\\left(2t+2\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{4\\left(2t+2\\right)}{6\\left(2{e}^{t}(t+1)\\right)}=\\frac{4\\left(1\\right)}{6\\left({e}^{t}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{4\\left(1\\right)}{6\\left({e}^{1}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\left(1\\right)}{6\\left(e\\right)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
