      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=3t e^{3t},\\quad y=5(t^2+2)e^{-3t},\\quad -\\infty<t<\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ as a function of $t$ for $t\\ne - \\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t\\right)\\right)e^{3t}+3t\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(e^{3t}\\right)\\right),\\quad y'(t)=5\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(t^2+2\\right)\\right)e^{-3t}+5(t^2+2)\\left(\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(e^{-3t}\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3e^{3t}+ 3t(3e^{3t}),\\quad y'(t)=5(2t)e^{-3t}+5(t^2+2)(-3e^{-3t})\n$$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3(1+3t)e^{3t},\\quad y'(t)=5(2t-3(t^2+2))e^{-3t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{5(2t-3(t^2+2))e^{-3t}}{3(1+3t)e^{3t}}=\\frac{5(2t-3(t^2+2))}{3(1+3t)}e^{-6t}.\n$$\n\nThis derivative is defined for $t\\ne - \\frac{1}{3}.$",
